{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Torque Unit Conversion.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Torque Unit Conversion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"torque unit conversion torque unit conversion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Torque Unit Conversion.md","fileName":"Torque Unit Conversion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Torque Unit Conversion","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Force Unit Conversion","Pressure Unit Conversion","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Torque Unit Conversion.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Torque Unit Conversion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"torque unit conversion torque unit conversion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Torque Unit Conversion.md","fileName":"Torque Unit Conversion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Torque Unit Conversion","heading":"Torque Unit Conversion","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A moment of force carries force multiplied by distance. Pryor converts pound-force feet to newton meters by applying a pound-force-to-newton factor and a feet-to-meters factor. Both original units cancel, leaving $\\mathrm{N\\,m}$. This differs from pressure, which divides force by area, even though both may begin with pound-force. A unit string such as lbf·ft should be kept intact until each part has been converted. See [[Force Unit Conversion]] and [[Pressure Unit Conversion]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Force Unit Conversion","Pressure Unit Conversion","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd"